    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0117",
      "sequence": 129,
      "meaning": "Ignorant beings fail to understand their own confusion and project delusion upon the Lord. Seeing a bank of clouds, foolish thinkers say it covers the sky; someone looking with a finger pressed against the eye imagines two visible moons. Delusion attributed to Rāma is like darkness, smoke or dust imagined to stain the sky. Sense objects, faculties, their deities and individual souls are each progressively conscious, but the supreme illuminator of all is beginningless Rāma, Lord of Ayodhyā. The world is what is illumined and Rāma its illuminator—master of māyā and abode of knowledge and virtue. Through his reality, inert māyā appears real with delusion’s aid, like silver seeming to lie in mother-of-pearl or water appearing in the sun’s rays, though false in all three times; no one can simply avert the illusion.",
