    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0118",
      "sequence": 130,
      "meaning": "Thus the world remains dependent upon Hari; though unreal, it produces suffering. If someone’s head is cut off in a dream, the pain does not end without waking. Girijā, the merciful Raghurāya is the one by whose grace this delusion vanishes. No one has found his beginning or end; reasoning from their understanding, the Vedas sing him as walking without feet, hearing without ears and performing countless acts without hands; without a mouth he enjoys every flavor, without speech he is a great speaker and yogī; bodiless he touches, eyeless he sees, and without a nose he receives every fragrance. His entire action is thus beyond the ordinary and his majesty indescribable. The same one whom Vedas praise and wise sages contemplate is Bhagavān, king of Kosala and son of Daśaratha, who assumed form for devotees’ welfare.",
