    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0120",
      "sequence": 132,
      "meaning": "Pārvatī says Śiva’s moonbeam-like words have dispelled the scorching autumn sun of delusion. Mercifully he removed every doubt and enabled her to know Rāma’s true nature. By his grace sorrow is gone and she is happy through the Lord’s feet. Although naturally dull, inexperienced and a woman, she asks him to recognize her as his servant and answer her original question if he is pleased: Rāma is imperishable, consciousness-made Brahman, detached from all yet dwelling in every heart-city—why did he assume a human body? Hearing Umā’s humble words and pure love for Rāma’s story, Kāma’s foe rejoices, praises her repeatedly, and begins: the stainless Lake of Rāma’s Deeds is the auspicious story Kākabhuśuṇḍi told Garuḍa; he will later recount that noble dialogue, but now she should hear the pure, beautiful incarnation story. Hari’s virtues and names are boundless and his stories and forms countless; Śiva will speak according to his understanding and Umā should listen reverently.",
